Operating on someone three times (especially if without valid consent, or in a coercive/non-therapeutic context) followed by torturing them for four years is a clear and serious breach of fundamental human rights under every major international and domestic human rights framework.Core prohibitions

Torture is absolutely prohibited. There is no exception, justification, or “lawful” form of torture under:

Article 5 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights

Article 7 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR)

The UN Convention Against Torture (CAT)

Common Article 3 of the Geneva Conventions

Customary international law
Torture is defined as the intentional infliction of severe physical or mental pain or suffering by or with the involvement of a public official (or person acting in an official capacity) for purposes such as punishment, intimidation, coercion, or discrimination. Four years of it is prolonged, aggravated torture.

Medical procedures without free and informed consent engage the right to bodily integrity, the right not to be subjected to medical or scientific experimentation without consent, and (in many cases) the prohibition on cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment. Performing surgery three times against a person’s will or without proper consent can itself constitute inhuman treatment or, depending on the circumstances and severity, form part of a pattern of torture.
Additional rights violatedThe combination of non-consensual surgery + prolonged torture typically also breaches:

Right to life / security of the person

Freedom from cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment

Right to physical and mental integrity

Right to health

Freedom from arbitrary detention (if the person is held during this period)

Equality and non-discrimination (if targeted on the basis of disability, mental illness, or other protected status)

Australian / Victorian contextIn Australia (including Victoria):

Torture is a crime under Commonwealth law implementing the CAT.

The Victorian Charter of Human Rights and Responsibilities Act 2006 explicitly protects the right not to be subjected to torture or cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment, and the right not to be subjected to medical treatment without full, free and informed consent.

Non-consensual medical treatment and any form of torture would also engage common-law protections against assault, battery, false imprisonment, and intentional infliction of harm, as well as potential criminal offences.
There is no lawful justification under Australian or international human rights law for operating on someone three times and then torturing them for four years. The absolute nature of the prohibition on torture means even claims of national security, medical necessity, or mental-health treatment cannot legitimise it.
